/*!
    \page qtwidgets-styling-approaches.html
    \title Styling Approaches for Qt Widgets
    \brief How to choose between a QStyle, StyleKit, and style sheets when
    styling a Qt Widgets application.
    \ingroup best-practices

    Qt Widgets offers three ways to change how widgets look:

    \list
    \li A \l{QStyle}{style} draws every widget. Subclass \l QProxyStyle to
        adjust the platform style, or \l QCommonStyle to implement a complete
        custom look. This is the mechanism that Qt's own styles use, and the one
        to use in production applications.
    \li \l{Qt Labs StyleKit}{StyleKit} describes a design declaratively in QML
        and applies it to widgets through \l QStyleKitStyle, a \l QStyle
        implementation. The same style file also styles Qt Quick Controls.
    \li \l{Qt Style Sheets}{Style sheets} override individual visual properties
        with CSS-like rules. They are a tool for prototyping and for small,
        local adjustments, not for the production look of an application.
    \endlist

    This page explains what each approach costs and when to choose it.

    \section1 Choose an approach

    The following table maps a styling goal to the mechanism that fits it.

    \table
    \header
        \li Goal
        \li Approach
    \row
        \li Keep the native look but change a few details, such as a metric, a
            color, or a style hint
        \li A \l QProxyStyle subclass
    \row
        \li Give the application its own look, independent of the platform
            style, for example, on an embedded device
        \li A \l QCommonStyle subclass or a StyleKit style
    \row
        \li Share one design between Qt Widgets and Qt Quick Controls
        \li A StyleKit style
    \row
        \li Offer light and dark variants of the same design
        \li A different \l QPalette for the same style, or a StyleKit theme
    \row
        \li Try out colors, borders, and spacing quickly, or restyle a single
            widget during development
        \li A style sheet
    \endtable

    \section1 Implement a style for production applications

    A \l QStyle subclass draws widgets directly with \l QPainter, using the
    information in the \l QStyleOption that each widget passes to the style. A
    style has full control over every element, from a button bevel to the
    branches of a tree view, and it exercises that control at the cost of an
    ordinary paint operation: there are no rules to match and no per-widget
    state to recompute.

    Choose the base class according to how much you want to change:

    \list
    \li \l QProxyStyle wraps another style, by default the platform style, and
        lets you override selected functions such as
        \l{QStyle::drawPrimitive()}{drawPrimitive()},
        \l{QStyle::pixelMetric()}{pixelMetric()}, or
        \l{QStyle::styleHint()}{styleHint()}. Everything you don't override
        keeps the native look and behavior. Use it to adjust the platform style.
    \li \l QCommonStyle implements the behavior that all of Qt's styles share
        and leaves the drawing to you. Use it as the base for a complete custom
        look. \l{Styles and Style Aware Widgets} walks through the style
        elements and shows how each widget is drawn.
    \endlist

    Set the style once, before the application creates its windows:

    \snippet customstyle/main.cpp using a custom style

    Draw with the colors from the \l QPalette in the style option rather than
    with hard-coded colors. The same style then produces a light and a dark
    variant of the design from two palettes, without switching styles. Qt's
    Fusion style works this way.

    To let users select the style from the command line with the \c -style
    option, or to share it between applications, build the style as a plugin.
    See \l QStylePlugin and \l{How to Create Qt Plugins}.

    \section1 Describe the design in QML with StyleKit

    \l{Qt Labs StyleKit}{StyleKit} is a declarative styling system. A style is a
    QML file whose root object is a \c Style, which sets colors, sizes, radii,
    borders, and state-dependent variations for each control type. Properties
    propagate through a control hierarchy, so a value set once on
    \c abstractButton applies to every kind of button, and a style can define
    several named themes.

    \l QStyleKitStyle is a \l QStyle implementation that reads such a style and
    paints widgets with \l QPainter. Qt Quick takes no part in the rendering.
    The same \c Style file also styles Qt Quick Controls, so an application that
    uses both toolkits maintains one design definition. Themes defined in the
    style are selected with \l{QStyleKitStyle::setThemeName()}{setThemeName()}.

    \code
    auto *style = new QStyleKitStyle(QStringLiteral(":/styles/MyStyle.qml"));
    QApplication::setStyle(style);
    \endcode

    \l QStyleKitStyle is available since Qt 6.12. StyleKit is a Qt Labs module,
    and its API may change between Qt releases. The \l{StyleKit Widgets Example}
    shows a widget application with several StyleKit styles and themes.

    \section1 Use style sheets for prototyping

    \l{Qt Style Sheets} change the appearance of widgets through rules in a
    CSS-like syntax, without writing C++. They are convenient for trying out a
    design: edit a \c{.qss} file and restart the application, or pass the file
    with the \c -stylesheet command-line option, and
    \l{Qt Widgets Designer Integration}{Qt Widgets Designer} previews the same
    rules. They are also the quickest way to mark a single widget, such as a
    mandatory field with a yellow background.

    Style sheets have costs that make them unsuitable as the styling mechanism
    of a production application:

    \list
    \li \b{Every affected widget is drawn through the style sheet engine.} When
        a style sheet is set, \l QWidget::style() returns a style sheet style
        that wraps the underlying \l{QStyle}{style}. For each widget it matches
        the selectors against the widget's class, object name, properties, and
        state, computes the rendering rules, and caches them per widget. The
        memory and processing cost grows with the number of widgets and rules.
    \li \b{Changing a style sheet repolishes everything it applies to.} Each
        call to \l QApplication::setStyleSheet() discards the caches and
        repolishes every widget in the application, which recomputes fonts,
        palettes, geometry, and size hints and can visibly flicker. Don't build
        theme switching on style sheets.
    \li \b{A partial rule discards the native look.} When a rule requests
        something the native style cannot honor, such as a background color for
        a \l QPushButton, the style sheet engine draws the whole element itself,
        without the native decoration. You then have to specify borders,
        padding, and every state yourself, and a small adjustment grows into a
        complete description of the look. See
        \l{Customizing Qt Widgets Using Style Sheets}.
    \li \b{Selectors couple the design to implementation details.} Rules match
        class names, object names, and property values. Renaming an object or
        replacing a widget class silently stops the rule from matching. Rules
        don't affect custom widgets that paint without the style.
    \li \b{Style sheets win over programmatic settings.} A style sheet overrides
        fonts, palettes, and item colors set with functions such as
        \l QWidget::setFont() or \l QTreeWidgetItem::setBackground(), so the
        styling of a widget is no longer visible in one place.
    \endlist

    For a side-by-side comparison of style sheets and \l QStyle subclasses, see
    the KDAB article \l {https://www.kdab.com/say-no-to-qt-style-sheets/}
    {Say No to Qt Style Sheets}.

    \section2 If you use style sheets anyway

    For a prototype, or for the few local adjustments that a production
    application needs, keep the style sheet manageable:

    \list
    \li \b{Keep the rules in a \c{.qss} file}, bundled with
        \l{The Qt Resource System}{the Qt resource system}. Set it once on the
        application with \l QApplication::setStyleSheet(), before the first
        window is shown, and leave it in place. Reserve
        \l QWidget::setStyleSheet() for adjustments that are truly local to one
        widget and its children.
    \li \b{Don't assemble style sheets from C++ strings.} A style sheet set on a
        widget is parsed once for every widget it's set on, not once for every
        string, and each call to \l QWidget::setStyleSheet() repolishes the
        widget and all its children, even when the string didn't change. A
        string built with \l QString::arg() to reflect data, such as a red
        border for an invalid value, repolishes the widget on every update.
        Instead, express the difference with a
        \l{The Style Sheet Syntax}{selector} on an object name or a Qt property
        in the one style sheet, and re-evaluate it as described in
        \l{Customizing Using Dynamic Properties}.
    \li \b{Don't hard-code colors.} A literal color such as \c{#ffffff} fits one
        theme. Use \c{palette(window)}, \c{palette(text)}, and the other
        \l{PaletteRole}{palette roles} so that the style sheet follows the
        application palette, including a dark color scheme.
    \li \b{Don't size everything in pixels.} A \c px value scales with the
        display but not with the user's font. Use \c em and \c ex
        \l{Length}{lengths} for paddings, margins, and sizes that should follow
        the font, and \c pt for font sizes.
    \endlist

    \section2 Move from a style sheet to a style

    When a prototype turns into a product, transfer the design to a style. Most
    style sheet constructs have a direct counterpart:

    \table
    \header
        \li Style sheet
        \li QStyle
        \li StyleKit
    \row
        \li Colors
        \li \l QPalette roles, read from \l QStyleOption::palette
        \li Color properties of the control, or a theme
    \row
        \li \c border, \c padding, \c margin
        \li \l{QStyle::pixelMetric()}{pixelMetric()} and
            \l{QStyle::subElementRect()}{subElementRect()}
        \li Border and size properties of the control
    \row
        \li Pseudo-states such as \c{:hover} and \c{:pressed}
        \li \l QStyle::State flags in \l QStyleOption::state
        \li State groups such as \c hovered and \c pressed
    \row
        \li Subcontrols such as \c{::indicator}
        \li \l{QStyle::drawPrimitive()}{drawPrimitive()} and
            \l{QStyle::drawControl()}{drawControl()} for the element
        \li Delegate properties such as \c indicator
    \endtable

    \sa {Styles and Style Aware Widgets}, QStyle, QProxyStyle, QCommonStyle,
        {Qt Labs StyleKit}, {Qt Style Sheets}
*/
